# Seat: Typography — Round 7 (working notes, written as I go)

Build: commit 984a0f17, frozen. Lens: type scale, 400/600 only, tracking curve,
leading, measure, mono micro-labels, numerals, orphans/widows, hierarchy.

## Log

### 1. Tracking survey (driven, 1440, all 10 states)
Probed computed letterfit for every visible text node, grouped by product
scope + family + size. Most of the audit's 10 "tracking" groups dissolve on
inspection — the curves compose a polarity branch (`--tr-13 + --tr-rev` =
-0.002em on the dock's reversed "Add task"; `--tr-12 + --tr-rev` = +0.002em on
"1 overdue"), which the audit does not model. Those are principled, not drift.

What survives:
- tasks · Geist Mono 11: `.trayCount` "3" is tracked +0.1em (the uppercase
  micro-label step) while `.pacePct` "38%" is tracked -0.012em. Two mono
  FIGURES in the same header band, 0.112em apart in letterfit.
- notes · Geist 12: `button.groupBtn` ("What it is about", "When") computes
  `normal` — the only 12px control in Notes off `--tr-12`.

### 2. Timeline · the date row below the axis is a staircase (CONFIRMED, measured)
across layout, `timeline.owner-flight`, widths 1024/1280/1440/1920 (identical
numbers at all four):
  ABOVE the axis  `.b-copy` is anchored by `bottom:` → `.b-date` top = 623 for
    every moment, whether the title ran one line or two. One clean row.
  BELOW the axis  `.b-copy` is anchored by `top:` → `.b-date` top = 752 for the
    one-line titles ("Send the invitations", "Venue walk-through") and 774 for
    the two-line one ("Choose the evening music"). A 22px step in a row of
    dates that is otherwise a ruler's own scale.
src/across.css:266-277 is where the two anchors are set.
Fix: reserve two lines for `.b-title` on the across axis and hug the
axis-facing edge, so `.b-copy` has one height on both sides.

### 3. tasks · the wrapping law — RAISED AND WITHDRAWN
tasks.css:923 gives `.cardTitle` two wrapping laws switched by a sibling
(`:has(.when)` → pretty, otherwise balance), four lines under a comment that
writes "THE RULE: no text block may have two wrapping laws switched by a
sibling." I measured both laws over 9 state×width combinations (board/dense/
compact × 1280/1440/1920), 142 multi-line titles:
  as-is             18 one-word LAST lines, 2 one-word FIRST lines, 0 over-clamp
  balance everywhere 8 one-word LAST lines, 9 one-word FIRST lines, 0 over-clamp
  pretty everywhere 24 one-word LAST lines, 14 short last lines
Balance-everywhere halves the widows but produces exactly the rag the comment
predicts at 1280 dense: «Chase» ⏎ «linen order, now» ⏎ «shipping Tuesday»,
«Gravel» ⏎ «delivered for» ⏎ «the top car park», five of them in one column.
So the recorded decision is the better of the two blunt options and my
re-measurement CONFIRMS it. NOT raised as a finding.
(What remains visible: 13 of the 18 widows are on chipped titles under
`pretty` — "Clear Sunday 11am late checkout with / housekeeping" breaks after
a preposition at 1280/1440/1920, and "Build the Saturday / run-sheet" at 1440.
Content-shaped, not fixable by a keyword.)

### 4. `.pacePct` — the one inherited letterfit in the suite (CONFIRMED)
Swept every visible text element in all 10 states for a letter-spacing
inherited across a size OR family change. Exactly one hit, suite-wide:
  tasks `span.pacePct` "38%" — Geist Mono 11px, ls = -0.13px, inherited from
  `.headFacts` (Geist 13px, `--tr-13` = -0.010em). So a MONO figure carries
  -0.0118em, a letterfit computed for 13px SANS.
tasks.css:325-328 declares family, size, line-height, weight, colour and
tabular figures for `.pacePct` and no letter-spacing. Tasks has no mono step;
the suite's declared one is +0.04em (notes `--tr-key`, timeline `--track-data`).
Meanwhile `.trayCount` (tasks.css:149) is a mono figure at +0.1em with
`margin-right:-0.1em` to kill the trailing space — so the two mono figures in
the same header band sit 0.112em apart in fit.
